{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Open</b> — four times in the Masorah: וכל כלי פתוח. שרשי פתוח אלי מים. קבר פתוח גרונם. אשפתו כקבר פתוח. This is what they said: four levels in an earthenware vessel — pierced so that liquid enters, disqualified from sanctifying hatat water in it, and this is the כלי פתוח stated here regarding hatat water, which is not considered a vessel. Yet it is still a vessel regarding seeds. Pierced like a small root of לוף (i.e., it is food of ravens), which is a species of pure legume, regarding seeds — for if seeds were sown in a perforated planter, it does not receive impurity, and this is שרשי פתוח אלי מים. Yet it is still a vessel regarding olives. Pierced so that an olive exits, pure from an ordinary earthenware vessel designated for olives, and this is פתוח גרונם, for ordinary eating is in an olive's volume. Yet it is still a vessel regarding pomegranates. Pierced so that a pomegranate exits, pure from all, and this is אשפתו כקבר פתוח — meaning, when it is pierced like a pomegranate and open so that a pomegranate exits, they throw it to the garbage, and it is no longer called a vessel."], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The Hebrew reads אשפתו כקבד פתוח in the fourth occurrence, but the earlier citation and the Masoretic text of Isaiah 30:13 read כקבר; translated per context as קבר. The final summary also writes כקבד — likely a typographical variant of קבר. The phrase טהור מכלום rendered 'pure from all' hyper-literally; possibly means 'pure altogether.'"}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:22:38.633416078Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}